$$\label{geo:distance}
 \lVert E_u-E_v\rVert_{\rm b}\ge d_*>0\qquad(u\ne v).$$ We decrease $d_*$ to a positive rational at most one. On any number of blocks of length $m$ use the inner product and norm $$\label{geo:norm}
 \langle U,V\rangle_{\rm b}=m^{-1}\sum_j U_jV_j,
 \qquad \lVert U\rVert_{\rm b}^2=m^{-1}\sum_j U_j^2.$$ An unqualified norm in this section means this norm. The infinity norm is unnormalized. Write $\mathcal B=[-1,2]^{4m}$, and let $\mathop{\mathrm{clip}}_{\mathcal B}$ denote coordinatewise clipping to this cube. Clipping is nonexpansive for <a href="#geo:norm" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="geo:norm">[geo:norm]</a>. Scalars occupying a block mean constant repetitions.

There is $\rho_{\rm loc}>0$, depending only on $d_*,a_0$, such that two points $Z,Z'\in\mathcal L$ with $\lVert Z-Z'\rVert<\rho_{\rm loc}$ are joined along one local straight or single-corner chain. Its length $\Delta$ satisfies $\Delta\le\sqrt2\lVert Z-Z'\rVert$. For every sufficiently small $\tau>0$, if $$\label{geo:tube-hypothesis}
 r=\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(X,\mathcal L)\le4\tau,\qquad
 \mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(X,\mathcal E\cup\{(H_*,2)\})>10\tau,$$ there is a unique closest point $Z$, which is interior and satisfies $\langle X-Z,T(Z)\rangle=0$. If $Z'\in\mathcal L$ has squared excess $g=\lVert X-Z'\rVert^2-r^2\le\tau^2$ and signed arclength difference $s'$ from $Z$ on that chain, then $$\label{geo:strong-excess}
 g\ge (s')^2/2.$$ In particular, $$\begin{aligned}
 \lVert Z'-Z\rVert&\le\sqrt{2g},&
 \lVert Z'-Z\rVert_\infty&\le C_t\sqrt{2g},\label{geo:point-error}\\
 \lVert T(Z')-T(Z)\rVert&\le\sqrt{2g}/a_0,&
 \lVert T(Z')-T(Z)\rVert_\infty&\le2C_t\sqrt{2g}/a_0.
 \label{geo:tangent-error}
\end{aligned}$$ The tangent at an endpoint competitor is its one-sided tangent.

