For a fixed $y$, the valid forward prefixes exist uniquely for an initial interval of lengths $0\le k\le k_{\mathrm f}(y)$ and equal $\operatorname{pref}_{\pi_{\mathrm f},k}(U_y)$. The valid reconstruction prefixes likewise exist uniquely for $0\le k\le k_{\mathrm r}(y)$ and equal $\operatorname{pref}_{\pi_{\mathrm r},k}(V_y)$. Moreover, $$\begin{aligned}
 k_{\mathrm f}(y)=h&\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad P(S(y))=y,
 \label{macro:forward-obstruction}\\
 k_{\mathrm r}(y)=h&\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad S(P(y))=y.
 \label{macro:reverse-obstruction}
\end{aligned}$$ If a maximal length is less than $h$, the next position is a designated output at which the additional comparison fails.

Set $$\label{oc:delta-one}
 \delta_1=\delta_0(\delta_0-\lambda_0)>0.$$ Every nonzero word of any of the four local codes, over any finite-dimensional $J$-space $U$, has at least $\delta_1d$ nonzero coordinates. There is a constant $C_0<\infty$, depending only on $d_0,\delta_0,\lambda_0$, with the following property for every $s_b$, every field and check matrices as above, every $U$, and every $s,t$. If $F$ is a $d\times d$ table with rows in $C_B^t(U)$ and $D$ is such a table with columns in $C_A^s(U)$, and their joint cell-disagreement fraction is $z$, there is a table $W$ whose rows and columns obey both codes and such that $$\label{oc:tensor-agreement}
 \Pr_i[W(i,\cdot)\ne F(i,\cdot)]
 +\Pr_j[W(\cdot,j)\ne D(\cdot,j)]\le C_0z.$$ In particular, $C_0$ is independent of $d$, $|J|$, and $\dim_JU$.

Fix the data in Theorem <a href="#thm:outer-codes" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:outer-codes">16</a>, and any finite tuple of full or A-only slots with their allowed A twists. Optionally include a control tuple constant over $\Gamma$ and canonical padding. There is a deterministic synchronous local correction map $\mathcal C$ with the following property. Let $V_0$ be any reference word satisfying all A local constraints and A agreements, with constant control and canonical parsing. Let $S\subseteq\Gamma$ and let $W$ be any input equal to $V_0$ outside $S$, with arbitrary values or malformed symbols on $S$. Then $\mathcal C W=V_0$ outside $$\label{oc:uncertainty-set}
 S'=
 \left\{k:\frac1d\#\{i:a_ik\in S\}\ge\delta_1/2\right\}
 \ \cup\
 \left\{k:\frac1d\#\{i:a_i^{-1}k\in S\}\ge\delta_1/2\right\}.$$ In particular, $S'$ depends on $S$ alone. If $u=|S|/|\Gamma|<\delta_1/4$, then $$\label{oc:uncertainty-shrink}
 \frac{|S'|}{|\Gamma|}
 \le\frac{2\lambda^2u}{(\delta_1/2-u)^2}\le u/2.$$ The local map uses a fixed number of permutation addresses and time polynomial in the symbol bit length and the public local-parameter length, with total default behavior on arbitrary inputs.*

*For every fixed integer $q\ge0$, the $q$-round map has a bounded input cone consisting of fixed permutations of its target; the bound may depend on $q$ but not on $N$. For an arbitrary initial $S$, the recursively defined supports contain the errors simultaneously for all completions on $S$. If $|S|/|\Gamma|<\delta_1/4$, their densities are at most $2^{-q}|S|/|\Gamma|$ after $q$ rounds. Different target-cone simulations may use different such completions, provided each simulation assigns one consistent initial value to every address repeated within that cone. Under this small-initial-support hypothesis, $O(\log|\Gamma|)$ rounds correct the whole word exactly in time polynomial in its explicit size and $N$.

There is a fixed $C_{\rm ar}$, determined by the finite direct-cone shape before $\beta$ is filled in, such that every entirely $\varepsilon$-satisfied direct cone obeys $$\label{out:coarse-pointwise}
 |X_{ir}-(A_{\rm rep}X)_{ir}|
       \leq2\beta B_1+C_{\rm ar}\varepsilon.$$ No correctness of its Boolean or functional inputs is required. If its direct comparisons and selected invocation answers are correct near-bits for the ideal run on $\bar X$, then also $$\label{out:fine-pointwise}
 |X_{ir}-\mathop{\mathrm{clip}}_{[-1,2]}((A_{\rm rep}X)_{ir}
                +\beta\widehat v_{ir}(\bar X))|
       \leq C_{\rm ar}\varepsilon.$$

If an assignment meets the gate-failure budget and $\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(\bar X,\mathcal E)>10\tau$, then $$\label{out:residual}
 E_{\rm res}:=
 \lVert X-\mathop{\mathrm{clip}}_{\mathcal B}(A_{\rm rep}X+\beta v(\bar X))\rVert_{\rm rep}
                  <\beta c_*\kappa/2.$$ Here clipping is componentwise on the replicas, and $v(\bar X)$ is repeated identically over them.

There is a polynomial-size extension field $J/\mathbb{F}_q$ and two $c_e$-by-$d_e$ matrices $H_L,H_R$ over $J$ whose every $c_e$-column minor is nonzero, such that the edge code $$\begin{aligned}
 \sum_i(H_L)_{bi}U_{i,g}&=0,\label{ib:left-row}\\
 \sum_i(H_R)_{bi}U_{i,u_i^{-1}x}&=0\label{ib:right-row}
\end{aligned}$$ has a unique linear extension $E$ from labels $i>2c_e$ to all labels. The same holds with symbols in any finite-dimensional $J$-space. Each local code has distance at least $c_e+1$, and $E$ commutes with every right shift $(R_hU)_{i,g}=U_{i,gh}$.

